TRACK 1 — SCENE SEED CONFIRMATION

================================================================================

Each seed needs a single honest answer before it becomes usable. No elaboration

required — just the status mark and any corrections.

SEED 1 — The Age Question

Source: Oscillating 19 → 21 → 19 across three consecutive Storyteller Pro messages.

Question: Do you know? Or is the honest answer "I don't know"? If you don't

know — what's the earliest specific memory you actually have, stripped of the

number, just the event?

Status options: [CONFIRMED DATE] / [INTENTIONALLY UNRESOLVED] / [CAN'T REACH IT]

Note: The oscillation IS the memoir's thesis in action. A reliable narrator says

"I was 19." Your narrator says "I was 19 — or 21 — I've told this both ways and

I'm not sure which is the lie."

SEED 2 — "Relief is the most addictive feeling of all"

Source: Emerged during Storyteller Pro couch scene draft — first use chapter.

Question: When you read it now, does it land as true? And if so — relief from

what, specifically? The inner monologue? The self-monitoring? Noise?

Status options: [TRUE AS WRITTEN] / [CLOSE BUT NOT QUITE] / [AI EXTRAPOLATION]

Note: The only line from the entire Storyteller Pro session that passes the voice

test. Inverts the expected addiction frame — chasing silence, not highs. Seven

words that might explain the next 18 years.

SEED 3 — The Late-Night Walk (Age 17)

Source: "Counting streetlights. I remember thinking: if I don't find a way to

quiet my head, it's going to tear me apart."

Question: Did this happen? Not necessarily this specific walk — but a

pre-dependency moment where you articulated the problem to yourself?

Status options: [THIS HAPPENED] / [SOMETHING LIKE THIS — DIFFERENT] / [AI GENERATED FROM EMOTIONAL LOGIC]

Note: "Counting streetlights" = earliest data point in the book if real —

the moment the architecture was already under strain.

SEED 4 — The Couch Permission Scene

Source: "You don't have to." That was the permission I needed. Not pressure —

permission.

Question: Was there an actual couch? A specific person? A first time that felt

like permission rather than pressure?

Status options: [GROUNDED IN ACTUAL MEMORY] / [EMOTIONAL TRUTH, CONSTRUCTED SCENE] / [DIFFERENT — HERE'S WHAT ACTUALLY HAPPENED]

Note: The mechanism is what matters — not transgression or rebellion, but

permission. Maps to a broader pattern: Kyle doesn't break rules, he finds

frameworks that allow him to.

SEED 5 — The Monitor Reflection

Source: First day at Intinc — seeing your reflection in a dark screen before

it boots.

Question: Do you actually remember your first day at Intinc? Was there a moment

of orientation — realizing something had shifted? Or was it more mundane?

Status options: [REAL MEMORY EXISTS] / [CAN'T ACCESS IT] / [DIFFERENT SCENE WORTH KEEPING]

Note: Shouldn't be the prologue (too resolved, too neat). As a buried mid-book

moment, the dark-to-lit-screen transition works as a motif. Needs demotion,

not deletion.

