TRACK 2 — BEFORE THE BREAK (Hospitality Years)

================================================================================

Raw excavation only. Sensory inventory. No meaning-making. No drafting.

THE ROOM — O'Toole's sensory inventory

What did it smell like?

What was the bar's layout? Where did you stand? Where were the regulars?

What time did shifts typically start and end?

What did "competent" feel like in your body when a shift was going well?

NOTE: Flag anything with visual weight as [SCENE POTENTIAL].

THE SKILL — Reading the room (locate it specifically)

What was the actual mechanism? How did you know when a table was about to turn

bad? When a regular was off?

Did you have a name for it at the time, or was it just something you did?

Was this a skill you were taught, or one you discovered you had?

Was it reliable? Did it ever fail badly?

NOTE: The book's argument is that you rebuilt this skill using external systems

after the break. Need to understand what the unassisted version felt like.

THE TIMELINE — Rough mapping (gaps are data)

O'Toole's / Bartending: [years? ages?]

Management: [years? ages?]

Quality Control: [years? ages?]

First interest in code: [when? what prompted it?]

First engineering job: [company? year?]

Intinc: [year?]

NOTE: Wherever uncertain, write "unclear." Gaps are not failures — they're data

about what the break took and what the reconstruction had to work around.

THE SIGNAL — Was there signal before the break?

Was there anything in the hospitality years that in retrospect reads as early

evidence of the strain?

A coping behavior that seemed functional at the time? A pattern of overcompensation?

The counting-streetlights equivalent — if that's real?

Or: nothing. If pre-break Kyle was genuinely functional, that's equally important.

NOTE: Not looking for a dramatic warning sign. If there was nothing — if the break

arrived without signal — that matters for the book's argument.

